session-workspace-arm4.txt -- the arm that came back INVALID, then SURVIVED
==============================================================================

    FlowVizWorkspaceModel.cpp  vs  FlowViz.UI.WorkspaceModel

    run 1  INVALID   -- did not compile
    run 2  SURVIVED  -- compiled, and the suite did not notice   <-- a real gap
    run 3  killed    -- after the assertion below was added

The identity control SURVIVED in runs 2 and 3, which is what makes those
verdicts mean anything.

WHAT THE ARM DOES
-----------------
It removes the `if (State.bCaseFound)` guard in FFlowVizWorkspaceModel::LoadState,
so a session whose case has moved calls OpenCase on the missing path instead of
reporting the relink failure directly.

RUN 1: INVALID, AND WHY THE FIX WAS NOT COSMETIC
------------------------------------------------
The original arm replaced the guard with `if (true)`. That does not compile:

    FlowVizWorkspaceModel.cpp:386:10: error: code will never be executed
                                      [-Werror,-Wunreachable-code]

The compiler proves the else dead, and this module builds with -Werror. An
INVALID is not a weak KILLED -- it is no verdict at all, and it is silent about
whether the tests could catch anything.

The replacement uses `if (!State.ResolvedCasePath.IsEmpty())`, true in exactly
the same cases (the enclosing guard has already established non-empty) but not
foldable, so the else stays formally reachable.

RUN 2: SURVIVED -- THE TEST'S RATIONALE WAS FALSE ABOUT THE CODE
-----------------------------------------------------------------
FlowViz.UI.WorkspaceModel.SessionMissingCase existed specifically to catch this
direction, and documented how:

    open the case anyway -> OpenCase on a path that does not exist fails, but
                            not before CloseCase has run, so the user loses the
                            case they had open.

OpenCase does not do that. It parses into a local, validates the codec, the
field and the grid, and reaches CloseCase only once every gate has passed --
the comment at the top of the function says so in as many words ("Parsed into a
local first"). A moved-away path fails at the FIRST gate. The open case survives
whether or not the guard exists, so `TestTrue(Workspace.IsCaseOpen())` passes
under both and cannot separate them.

Neither can the other two assertions. Both implementations return FileNotFound,
and both name the same path -- the manifest reader reports the path it was
handed. Three assertions, all of them true of the mutant.

This is a-finding-names-an-instance in an unusual form: the defect was not in
the coordinates the arm touched, it was in the PROSE that told a reader this
branch was covered. A comment naming the mechanism that handles a hazard turns
the auditor away (prose-claiming-a-hazard-is-solved), and here the comment was
simply wrong about the mechanism.

RUN 3: WHAT ACTUALLY DISTINGUISHES THEM
---------------------------------------
The message. The guarded branch says the case can be relinked and everything
else was applied; the unguarded one degrades to the manifest reader's "manifest
not found", which reads as a failed open and offers a relink prompt nothing to
say. Since the prompt is written from that text, the text is now asserted -- on
substrings ("relink", "everything else was applied") rather than the full
sentence, so a copy-edit is not a test failure.

`IsCaseOpen()` was kept but relabelled as what it is: a pin on OpenCase's
validate-then-commit ordering, which the relink flow depends on, and not the
discriminator it was described as.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER
------------------------
The relink flow itself. FlowVizSession::RelinkCase is tested at the session
layer, but nothing drives the full load -> discover missing -> relink -> apply
sequence through FFlowVizWorkspaceModel, and no UI offers it (task #53).
